  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nHigh cloud coverage continues, but is expected to thin out Tuesday.\nPrecipitable water values are expected to remain elevated which \nwill keep overnight lows elevated and conditions feeling tropical.\nThe tropical feeling airmass is expected to disrupt marine layer \nlow cloud and fog development and may result in a weaker afternoon\nsea breeze. This will contribute to high and low temperatures \nalong the coast running 10 to 15 degrees above average. Further \ninland, daytime highs will be 5 to 10 degrees above average with a\nmore notable increase in low temperatures which are expectd to \nrun 8 to 15 degrees above average. Hottest conditions are expected\nTuesday through Thursday for the coast, valleys, and mountains \nbelow 5000 ft with hottest conditions expected in the low desert \nWednesday through Friday. Widespread moderate to areas of major \nHeatRisk are expected. \n\nThe lingering moisture and increased sunshine for Tuesday and \nWednesday will increase chances of afternoon showers and \nthunderstorms over the mountains. There is expected to be little to \nno additional forcing outside of the usual diurnal heating so \ncoverage of any thunderstorms should be fairly isolated. By \nThursday, a transient ridge of high pressure is expected to move \ndirectly over Southern California. This would result in an airmass \nlikely too stable for thunderstorm development, although with \nlingering moisture in the air a few showers cannot be ruled out over \nthe mountains. \n\nThere is a spread in potential outcomes for the upper level pattern \nover the upcoming weekend. 32 percent of ensemble solutions have a \ntrough over the Pacific Northwest, which would bring noticeable \nrelief from the heat and humidity. 17 percent of solutions keep the \nridge overhead which would prolong the heat event. The rest of the \nsolutions are somewhere in between, with a slower passage of the \ntrough but a weakening of the ridge. Current forecast follows the \nNBM and is more inline with the \"in between\" solutions with a \ngradual decrease in both high and low temperatures Friday through \nSunday.",
